# power.schema — which layer may decide each key of the `power` subsystem.
#
# Read by /usr/lib/w/w-conf-lib.sh (and its Python twin) during the load
# pass; a user-layer assignment to a `system` key is dropped with a warning. This
# replaces folklore that used to live inside w-power's head ("idle timers may be
# overridden per user, lid/charge may not") with one declaration that w-power,
# the Hub, the AI tools and `w-conf` all obey. See update-system.md (decision 3).
#
# Format: <KEY>\t<scope>       scope: system | user | both
#   system — root domain: the admin (or fleet policy) decides, per machine.
#   user   — the logged-in user may override it in ~/.config/w/power.conf.
#   both   — either layer may speak; the usual priority order applies.
#
# The values themselves come from the generated vendor layer
# (/usr/share/w/defaults/power.conf, written by w-power from preset_sys/
# preset_idle) — this file only says WHO may change them.

# ── Machine mode: the selector that chooses the whole preset table ────────────
MODE	system

# ── Power profiles (power-profiles-daemon) ───────────────────────────────────
DEFAULT_PROFILE	system
AUTO_PROFILE	system
PROFILE_ON_AC	system
PROFILE_ON_BATTERY	system

# ── Battery / lid / power key: hardware policy, root domain ──────────────────
CHARGE_LIMIT	system
LID_ON_BATTERY	system
LID_ON_AC	system
LID_DOCKED	system
LID_WAKE	system
POWER_KEY	system

# ── Idle cascade: the user's own session, per user ───────────────────────────
# Tweaking "lock my screen after N seconds" has never needed root, and the two
# power sources are separate because a laptop on battery wants a tighter cascade.
AC_LOCK	user
AC_DISPLAY	user
AC_SUSPEND	user
BAT_LOCK	user
BAT_DISPLAY	user
BAT_SUSPEND	user

# ── Keyboard backlight off on idle ───────────────────────────────────────────
# Same domain as the cascade above (my session, my timers, no root), but NOT a
# cascade link: an independent listener counting from last activity. The level,
# the device and the boot-time light are a different subsystem — see
# /usr/share/w/defaults/kbdlight.schema — this is only "when does it go dark".
AC_KBDLIGHT	user
BAT_KBDLIGHT	user
